1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,230
You take home a brand-new twelve-million-dong phone without paying cash today.

2
00:00:04,230 --> 00:00:07,190
The flyer advertises zero-percent installment financing...

3
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:11,370
but does any lender extend capital for an entire year without collecting interest?

4
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:20,160
The moment you sign, the installment agreement does not stay with the store; it routes directly to an independent consumer finance company.

5
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:25,650
From that second, your legal creditor is the finance company, though zero funds have moved yet.

6
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:33,380
The next morning, the finance company disburses funds to the store, but look closely: only eleven million four hundred thousand dong arrives.

7
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:39,770
The missing six hundred thousand dong withheld at disbursement is the five-percent interest subsidy the merchant quietly pays for you.

8
00:00:40,730 --> 00:00:44,380
In return, you repay one million dong each month for twelve months.

9
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:51,240
If an upfront cash buyer gets a six hundred thousand discount, you paid twelve million for an eleven point four million phone.

10
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,410
Your effective interest rate is zero point eight percent monthly, roughly ten percent annually.

11
00:00:57,170 --> 00:01:00,150
What if cash and installment prices are identical?

12
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:09,250
Then you pay zero interest, and that six hundred thousand dong fee is absorbed entirely by the store, funded out of retail markups to drive sales volume.

13
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:15,830
The next time you see zero percent financing, ask one essential question: what is the cash price if I pay in full today?

14
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:18,050
The discount that appears is the real interest.
